1
00:00:02,940 --> 00:00:05,160
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:05,920
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:10,820
My name is Catherine and today we've got another lesson all about food and eating.

4
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:14,420
So today we're going to talk about all the tools that we use when we're at the table.

5
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:16,620
So what we use when we're eating.

6
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:17,540
That's right.

7
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:23,980
So let's take a listen to today's dialogue and when we come back, we'll talk about some of these words and some of the great phrases you're learning today.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,200
Honey, can you set the table?

9
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:33,200
Well, I'm sure.

10
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:34,200
What are we having for dinner?

11
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:36,270
Do I need to put anything out in particular?

12
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:39,290
Well, make sure to put out the pepper and salt shakers.

13
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:43,880
I don't know if your brother is coming tonight, so set an extra place mat just in case.

14
00:00:44,110 --> 00:00:44,510
Okay.

15
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:45,930
Should I use the fancy silverware?

16
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:46,910
Yeah, go ahead.

17
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:48,410
Forks, spoons, and knives.

18
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:52,490
I roasted some meat, so be sure to put out some steak knives as well.

19
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:55,470
I'll also set some cups and saucers for some coffee after dinner.

20
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,220
Honey, have you seen our soup bowls?

21
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:01,220
They're in the cupboard where you keep the gravy boat and serving dishes.

22
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,660
Just be careful because the wine glasses are also there.

23
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:08,400
Oops.

24
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,620
All right, we're back.

25
00:01:12,620 --> 00:01:15,590
So now let's take a look at a couple of words in language takeaway.

26
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:18,950
Language takeaway.

27
00:01:19,790 --> 00:01:22,510
So our first word is a very important one.

28
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:24,680
You might have heard it before.

29
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:25,890
The word is silverware.

30
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:27,550
Okay, silverware.

31
00:01:27,770 --> 00:01:31,990
Now this means that it's usually made something that's made out of silver, right?

32
00:01:32,250 --> 00:01:32,690
It could be.

33
00:01:32,930 --> 00:01:35,710
Maybe traditionally it's made out of silver, but it doesn't have to be.

34
00:01:35,770 --> 00:01:38,590
You could have wooden silverware or other metals.

35
00:01:39,210 --> 00:01:43,150
It's basically the things that you use to eat food with.

36
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:46,060
Okay, so we're going to take this as a category.

37
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:52,000
There are all the tools that we use to eat food with.

38
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,940
So for example, a fork is a part of a silverware.

39
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:57,920
It's a kind of silverware, fork.

40
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:04,020
A fork is the one that you usually put in your mouth with a piece of lettuce or meat or something.

41
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:05,450
Okay, so it's pointy.

42
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:08,120
It has like four little points.

43
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:13,770
Now the one that we use to have dessert or to have soup with, we call that a spoon.

44
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:15,370
A spoon, that's right.

45
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:18,350
So to eat yogurt or ice cream, you use a spoon.

46
00:02:19,170 --> 00:02:21,610
And finally, to cut things, you use a knife.

47
00:02:22,090 --> 00:02:28,250
Okay, so in the dialogue we saw the plural form, knives, but singular it would be knife.

48
00:02:28,830 --> 00:02:29,290
That's right.

49
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:32,840
And sometimes there are foods that are very, very hard to cut.

50
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:36,530
For example, steak is very hard to cut because it's a tough meat.

51
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:39,270
And so in that case, we use a steak knife to cut it.

52
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:42,370
Okay, so we have a knife or a steak knife.

53
00:02:42,730 --> 00:02:45,930
Steak knife more specifically is used to cut meats.

54
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,280
All right, so these are three kinds or actually four kinds of silverware.

55
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,760
Of course, there are other kinds, but those are the most important.

56
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:58,240
After that though, we have something that's important for making your food taste better.

57
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:00,360
So what if I want to add salt or pepper?

58
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:01,540
What are those things called?

59
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:04,300
Those are called salt and pepper shakers.

60
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:09,820
We saw them in the dialogue that we need to put out the pepper and salt shakers.

61
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:13,080
So the little thing where you keep the salt is called a shaker.

62
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:16,730
That's right, because the action, the verb is to shake.

63
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:21,680
That means moving your hand up and down so some salt will fall out onto your food.

64
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:22,680
Exactly.

65
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:27,280
So usually it's only salt and pepper that we keep in these things, in these little shakers.

66
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:35,870
But apart from salt and pepper shakers and all the silverware we're putting out, we're also putting out some saucers because we're going to have coffee later on.

67
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:41,520
Okay, so this normally goes together with cups, cups and saucers, but everyone knows what a cup is.

68
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:42,380
What is a saucer?

69
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:47,440
So a saucer is this little plate that you put under the cup when you're having coffee.

70
00:03:47,720 --> 00:03:51,420
So usually that's where you keep your spoon or maybe the little bag of tea.

71
00:03:52,020 --> 00:04:00,720
Okay, so a saucer is important because it also helps in case you spill some coffee or tea so the coffee or tea doesn't go on the ground, it goes on the plate.

72
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:01,160
Exactly.

73
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:04,360
So that little plate is called a saucer.

74
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:07,340
Now where do we keep all the plates, all the silverware?

75
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:09,800
We usually keep them in a cupboard.

76
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:16,320
Okay, so you might want to say cupboard because it's cup, C-U-P-B-O-A-R-D.

77
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:16,860
That's not right.

78
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:19,140
The word is pronounced cupboard.

79
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:26,840
Okay, so even though it may seem like you should pronounce cupboard, the correct pronunciation is cupboard.

80
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:27,840
Cupboard.

81
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:28,840
Cupboard.

82
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:38,050
And a cupboard, like you said, is usually in the dining room or the kitchen because it houses, it keeps all of our plates and bowls and sometimes our silverware.

83
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:38,990
That's right.

84
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:42,850
Okay, so actually in the cupboard we also have some other things, dishes.

85
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:46,370
Specifically we have a gravy boat in there.

86
00:04:46,650 --> 00:04:52,570
Okay, so I'm not even sure why they call it a boat, but a gravy boat is basically a special dish.

87
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:59,090
It's like a tall bowl with a spout so you can pour it that keeps gravy.

88
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:02,850
So gravy is like a liquid or a sauce that goes on top of meat or potatoes.

89
00:05:03,330 --> 00:05:04,950
I guess it kind of looks like a boat.

90
00:05:05,230 --> 00:05:08,550
Just imagine a boat, but inside it's empty.

91
00:05:08,830 --> 00:05:10,030
So then that's where you put the gravy in.

92
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:11,440
You pour it.

93
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:12,440
You can pour it.

94
00:05:12,440 --> 00:05:13,670
All right, and so we can find that.

95
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:23,110
We can also find serving dishes, which are basically big plates or bowls that we use when we're having dinner at the dinner table to keep, for example, a turkey or a roast.

96
00:05:24,760 --> 00:05:27,510
And specifically we were also looking for wine glasses.

97
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:35,030
Well, maybe we weren't looking for them, but they found us because the person getting stuff out of the cupboard dropped some wine glasses.

98
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,360
These are very, very easy to break.

99
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:44,140
Wine glasses are basically just glasses that maybe are tall and you drink wine out of them.

100
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:44,680
That's right.

101
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:48,960
So there are specific type of glass usually used to drink wine.

102
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:55,440
So wine glasses, I guess a normal glass that you use to drink maybe juice or water is just called a glass.

103
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:56,020
Right.

104
00:05:56,840 --> 00:05:59,200
So you can't use a wine glass only when you're drinking wine.

105
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:00,200
Okay.

106
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:05,380
So we've taken a look at a lot of vocab here, a lot of tools that we use at the dinner table when we're having dinner.

107
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:08,480
So let's listen to the dialogue again and review.

108
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:15,040
Honey, can you set the table?

109
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:15,940
Sure.

110
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:17,080
What are we having for dinner?

111
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,320
Do I need to put anything out in particular?

112
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:22,320
Well, make sure to put out the pepper and salt shakers.

113
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:24,120
I don't know if your brother is coming tonight.

114
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,960
So set an extra place mat just in case.

115
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:27,540
Okay.

116
00:06:27,720 --> 00:06:28,960
Should I use the fancy silverware?

117
00:06:29,280 --> 00:06:29,960
Yeah, go ahead.

118
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:31,420
Forks, spoons, and knives.

119
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:35,520
I roasted some meat, so be sure to put out some steak knives as well.

120
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:38,480
I'll also set some cups and saucers for some coffee after dinner.

121
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:41,290
Honey, have you seen our soup bowls?

122
00:06:41,830 --> 00:06:44,270
They're in the cupboard where you keep the gravy boat and serving dishes.

123
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:47,800
Just be careful because the wine glasses are also the...

124
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:48,800
Oops.

125
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:49,800
All right.

126
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:53,840
We're back.

127
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:58,500
So now we have four key phrases that we've prepared for you on Fluency Builder.

128
00:07:00,710 --> 00:07:01,570
Fluency Builder.

129
00:07:02,730 --> 00:07:03,230
Okay.

130
00:07:03,250 --> 00:07:05,850
So this first phrase is very important, especially as a child.

131
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:08,600
Your mother or father might ask you to do this.

132
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:10,170
The phrase is to set the table.

133
00:07:10,650 --> 00:07:10,950
Okay.

134
00:07:11,010 --> 00:07:14,750
So that's a phrasal verb, to set the table.

135
00:07:14,950 --> 00:07:17,790
And what does it mean if you're asked to set the table?

136
00:07:18,070 --> 00:07:23,150
It means you will put down the plates and the silverware and the bowls so that everything is prepared for dinner.

137
00:07:23,610 --> 00:07:23,930
Okay.

138
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:29,540
So this is actually what we were doing and a complementary phrasal verb to this.

139
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:38,180
When you're setting the table, as you said, you are putting down or putting out the different spoons, knives, dishes, all that stuff.

140
00:07:38,640 --> 00:07:38,860
Right.

141
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:46,520
So the verbal phrase to put out means to place down or to make sure that you get something out on the table.

142
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:51,800
In this case, my mother would usually ask me to put out extra glasses for water.

143
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:52,440
Okay.

144
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:55,840
Or don't forget to put out the wine glasses in case we're drinking wine.

145
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:56,760
It just means to place down.

146
00:07:57,020 --> 00:07:57,440
Exactly.

147
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:02,860
So just remember that we use it because all these things, they're inside the cupboard.

148
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:04,160
So you have to take them out.

149
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:06,340
So you have to put them out on the table.

150
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:07,880
All right.

151
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:22,440
And sometimes we may have a guest or we may need an extra chair or an extra seat because we are having company and the person in dialogue said to set an extra placemat just in case.

152
00:08:22,950 --> 00:08:23,230
Okay.

153
00:08:23,370 --> 00:08:30,770
So placemat is basically a piece of plastic or maybe fabric that you put in front of a person when they're at the table.

154
00:08:30,990 --> 00:08:34,790
So it's on the table and it's something that protects the table.

155
00:08:35,350 --> 00:08:35,870
So it's small.

156
00:08:36,190 --> 00:08:40,190
Everyone has one and the bowl and the silverware will go on top of that placemat.

157
00:08:40,590 --> 00:08:43,050
But it also indicates that that is where someone will sit.

158
00:08:43,690 --> 00:08:43,830
Okay.

159
00:08:43,890 --> 00:08:51,510
So to set out or to set out an extra placemat means to prepare an extra place for someone else who will be coming.

160
00:08:51,890 --> 00:08:55,850
So if you have a guest, for example, you'll set out an extra placemat for him or for her.

161
00:08:56,110 --> 00:08:56,470
Okay.

162
00:08:56,490 --> 00:09:07,490
So we can say set an extra placemat because this is by this we are referring that somebody is going to sit down at the table or you can just simply say set an extra place for someone.

163
00:09:07,790 --> 00:09:08,210
That's right.

164
00:09:08,410 --> 00:09:08,990
Set an extra place.

165
00:09:08,990 --> 00:09:11,330
Your older brother is coming home from college tonight.

166
00:09:11,610 --> 00:09:11,890
Okay.

167
00:09:12,760 --> 00:09:14,380
So you set an extra place for him at the table.

168
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:18,820
And well, in this case, we didn't know if the brother was really coming or not.

169
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:20,960
So she said just in case.

170
00:09:21,860 --> 00:09:24,060
This is a great phrase, one that you'll hear all the time.

171
00:09:24,840 --> 00:09:28,240
It means I'm not sure what the result will be or what the outcome will be.

172
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:32,790
But in case something happens or just in case we should do this.

173
00:09:33,270 --> 00:09:35,530
So for example, Marco, I think it's going to rain today.

174
00:09:35,690 --> 00:09:37,610
You should bring an umbrella just in case.

175
00:09:37,950 --> 00:09:38,250
Okay.

176
00:09:38,330 --> 00:09:41,770
So this is a way of saying you're preventing something.

177
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:42,390
You're prepared.

178
00:09:43,050 --> 00:09:45,850
Maybe if it does rain, you'll have an umbrella just in case.

179
00:09:46,050 --> 00:09:46,490
Bring your umbrella.

180
00:09:47,510 --> 00:09:47,630
Okay.

181
00:09:47,790 --> 00:09:49,490
So a lot of great phrases there.

182
00:09:49,930 --> 00:09:50,790
A lot of vocab.

183
00:09:50,890 --> 00:09:53,450
So why don't we listen to the dialogue for the last time.

184
00:10:00,250 --> 00:10:01,530
Honey, can you set the table?

185
00:10:02,370 --> 00:10:02,770
Sure.

186
00:10:03,170 --> 00:10:03,890
What are we having for dinner?

187
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:06,120
Do I need to put anything out in particular?

188
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:09,140
Well, make sure to put out the pepper and salt shakers.

189
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:10,900
I don't know if your brother is coming tonight.

190
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:13,200
So set an extra placemat just in case.

191
00:10:13,960 --> 00:10:14,340
Okay.

192
00:10:14,460 --> 00:10:15,760
Should I use the fancy silverware?

193
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:16,740
Yeah, go ahead.

194
00:10:17,120 --> 00:10:18,220
Forks, spoons, and knives.

195
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:22,320
I roasted some meat, so be sure to put out some steak knives as well.

196
00:10:22,760 --> 00:10:25,360
I'll also set some cups and saucers for some coffee after dinner.

197
00:10:26,290 --> 00:10:28,050
Honey, have you seen our soup bowls?

198
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:31,540
They're in the cupboard where you keep the gravy boat and serving dishes.

199
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:33,490
Just be careful because the wine glasses are also there.

200
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:35,640
Oops.

201
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:43,920
I'll be right back.

202
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:44,920
All right, we're back.

203
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:49,440
So talking about dinner and silverware, there are also a couple of other names that we didn't mention.

204
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:53,580
For example, many people refer to ceramic plates as china.

205
00:10:54,320 --> 00:11:00,040
Yeah, these are usually the fancy kinds of plates and dishes that you have in your house.

206
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:09,180
Obviously, if you have just kind of the cheap ceramics that you can throw away or even paper plates, those aren't very important.

207
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:15,320
But at a wedding or in a special occasion, a person or a family might receive chinaware, china.

208
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:21,840
And that is very nice, very pretty porcelain plates or just lacquerware.

209
00:11:22,210 --> 00:11:30,030
Okay, so you can call it chinaware, which is basically plates that are either very fine ceramic or porcelain, as you said.

210
00:11:30,130 --> 00:11:30,350
That's right.

211
00:11:30,790 --> 00:11:32,410
Or what was the other word that you said before?

212
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:34,080
Lacquerware.

213
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:39,200
Lacquerware is a certain kind of plate or ceramic that has a kind of sheen to it.

214
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:42,920
It's very special, kind of basically paint job.

215
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:43,150
Oh, okay.

216
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:52,730
Yeah, because it's always been kind of strange the fact that a name like china would also now be related to plates.

217
00:11:52,960 --> 00:12:02,960
But apparently, I guess because Chinese porcelain is renowned as being such high quality, I guess this is why now we just have a word for it called china or chinaware.

218
00:12:02,960 --> 00:12:12,410
I think it actually has to do with the fact that the Chinese in the old days sold, well, most of the British bought their porcelain from China.

219
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:15,350
And so it was very, very in vogue, it was very popular.

220
00:12:15,970 --> 00:12:20,010
And it was basically the only thing that the British wanted.

221
00:12:20,210 --> 00:12:21,210
They just wanted all of this china.

222
00:12:21,690 --> 00:12:24,090
And it became a part of the vocabulary then.

223
00:12:24,330 --> 00:12:27,410
Yeah, it's really strange though that you would call something china.

224
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:32,650
It's like, I don't know any other country that you call something, you know, like French.

225
00:12:33,540 --> 00:12:35,800
This is a French or Frenchware.

226
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:36,800
I don't know.

227
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:38,400
I can't think of any.

228
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:42,440
Limoges is the name of a city in France and it's a certain kind of ceramics.

229
00:12:42,440 --> 00:12:43,310
Oh, there you go.

230
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:44,170
All right.

231
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:45,790
So an interesting topic.

232
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:52,230
There are many other tools that we didn't mention like salad fork and ice cream spoon.

233
00:12:52,880 --> 00:12:53,210
I don't know.

234
00:12:53,880 --> 00:13:00,640
And sometimes if you go to a fancy dinner, you will have maybe three spoons, three knives, three forks and you don't know what to do with it.

235
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:02,000
Start on the outside and go in.

236
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:03,200
That's my one recommendation.

237
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:03,980
